-- title:
--   Unimodular pairs mod n lift to coprime integer pairs
-- statement:
--   Let $n, c, d$ be integers, and suppose that the ideal generated by $c$, $d$ and $n$ is the unit ideal, in the explicit form that there exist integers $x, y, z$ with $xc + yd + zn = 1$. The conclusion is that there exist integers $c'$ and $d'$ with $c' \equiv c \pmod n$ and $d' \equiv d \pmod n$ (congruence in the sense of `Int.ModEq`, written `[ZMOD n]`) such that `IsCoprime c' d'` holds, i.e. there are integers $a, b$ with $a c' + b d' = 1$. No hypothesis $n \neq 0$ is imposed: when $n = 0$ the two congruences are equalities and the hypothesis itself exhibits a Bézout relation for $c$ and $d$ after absorbing the term $z \cdot 0$. Nothing is asserted about the size of $c'$ and $d'$, and no completion to a matrix of determinant one is produced.
--
--   This is the elementary lifting lemma underlying the surjectivity of the reduction map $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}) \to \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})$: a pair unimodular modulo $n$ is congruent to a genuinely coprime pair, which then completes to an integral matrix of determinant one by Bézout. It is used in the treatment of Hecke operators on modular forms, for instance when choosing explicit $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$-representatives of classes of matrices prescribed modulo the level.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Int_exists_modEq_and_modEq_and_isCoprime.lean

import Mathlib.Data.Int.GCD
import Mathlib.Data.Int.ModEq
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Coprime.Lemmas

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
set_option synthInstance.maxHeartbeats 400000
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency.types false

theorem Int.exists_modEq_and_modEq_and_isCoprime {n c d : ℤ}
    (h : ∃ x y z : ℤ, x * c + y * d + z * n = 1) :
    ∃ c' d' : ℤ, c' ≡ c [ZMOD n] ∧ d' ≡ d [ZMOD n] ∧ IsCoprime c' d' := by sorry
